    {
      "id": "lesson:creative:decision-contract",
      "slug": "decision-contract",
      "title": "Start with the decision, not the visual",
      "explanation": "A creative pattern earns its place by changing a defensible decision. Specify the audience, decision, observation grain, metric definition, comparison and action before selecting a renderer. The Shortlist has three active experiments and three reserves; this is the owner’s portfolio limit, not a universal Power BI rule. Its acceptance rule asks about decision value, a simpler alternative, reusability and ownership cost. A pattern can fail this rule despite looking impressive. Keep the proposed business benefit separate from an observed outcome; none of the three active Shortlist ideas is recorded as implemented.",
      "objectives": [
        "Write a falsifiable decision brief",
        "Separate a useful analysis from a decorative feature"
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Choose a named decision owner and the action they can actually take.",
        "Write: I need to identify [condition] among [entities] within [time], so I can [action].",
        "Specify the grain, numerator/denominator and comparison population; record missing fields instead of inventing them.",
        "Build or specify a simple native baseline with the same information.",
        "Evaluate the candidate against that baseline: correct decisions, interpretation errors, interactions and measured performance.",
        "Record Keep, Revise or Drop with actual evidence after the experiment; leave the result pending before execution."
      ],
      "misconceptions_and_tradeoffs": [
        "A prettier report does not establish a better decision.",
        "A numerical target suggested in the Field Guide is a proposed test criterion, not a Microsoft SLA."
      ],
      "practice": "An operations manager asks for a new animated late-delivery map but cannot name a follow-up action. Write the missing decision contract and choose a simpler baseline.",
      "model_feedback": "First clarify who escalates which shipment and what evidence sets priority. A ranked exception table may be enough. Keep animation optional until it adds demonstrable analytical value.",
