    {
      "id": "lesson:creative:distribution",
      "slug": "distribution",
      "title": "Distribution Detective: build and challenge the bins",
      "explanation": "The same mean can describe different service processes. A histogram groups individual observations into numeric intervals; the interval boundaries are part of the analysis. The linked W39 challenge uses clinic waiting-time practice data, not independently authenticated clinical observations. Its source requests disconnected width and bin-axis tables and a count measure so the reader can vary width. Built-in Power BI binning is a separate, fixed-field grouping feature: adding a slicer does not automatically make that field’s bin size dynamic. Use half-open intervals [start, start + width) so a boundary observation belongs to exactly one bin. For unequal widths, frequency height is misleading: density must preserve area meaning.",
      "objectives": [
        "Explain why a mean can hide a tail or multiple modes",
        "Specify nonoverlapping dynamic bins and a conservation test"
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Retrieve the pinned W39 CSV using the reference receipt; obtain reuse permission before redistributing it.",
        "Verify 2,400 records, four expected columns and unique CaseID; keep dates and Location filters.",
        "Create disconnected width choices 1, 2, 5, 10 and an integer bin-start axis 0–45, as the challenge specifies.",
        "Implement the derived count measure in CODE/ with explicit width selection and half-open bounds.",
        "Use a column chart, bin start on X and count on Y; compare Location with small multiples and shared comparison scales.",
        "Set touching-column formatting where the installed version supports it; retain a clear interval label.",
        "For each width and location/date selection, compare the sum of visible bin counts with the eligible row count; inspect observations outside the axis range.",
        "Compare shapes at multiple widths before choosing a conclusion; a smoothed or broad bin can hide a real feature."
      ],
      "misconceptions_and_tradeoffs": [
        "A categorical bar chart counts categories; a histogram groups numeric intervals.",
        "Zero for a valid empty bin differs from blank for a non-grid position.",
        "Native built-in binning and a DAX-controlled interval grid are two implementations, not interchangeable UI settings."
      ],
      "practice": "Why might a two-peak pattern disappear when width changes from 1 to 5? What else must stay fixed to compare the two sites?",
      "model_feedback": "Coarser bins can merge distinct groups. Keep the eligible observations, numeric range, units and comparison scales consistent, and report the chosen width.",
      "prerequisite_slugs": [
        "decision-contract"
      ],
      "prerequisite_lesson_ids": [
        "lesson:creative:decision-contract"
      ],
