    {
      "id": "lesson:creative:metric-map",
      "slug": "metric-map",
      "title": "Metric Navigator: reconcile identities before explaining causes",
      "explanation": "A metric map is a calculation graph whose branches explain how totals are built. A signed additive identity reconciles a parent change to signed child changes. For a product A×B, the W40 source gives a midpoint split: ΔA times the average of B’s two-period values, and ΔB times the average of A’s values. The contributions sum algebraically to the product change. This is an accounting decomposition, not evidence that a component caused a business outcome. Share a measure definition across repeated branches while forbidding cycles along an ancestor path. A generic decomposition tree is a useful native comparison baseline, but its dimension splits are not automatically a renderer of arbitrary formula operators.",
      "objectives": [
        "Separate a calculation dependency from a causal edge",
        "Test additive and multiplicative reconciliation in a common filter context"
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Write the measure dictionary, units, sign conventions, and valid filters before designing nodes.",
        "At the order level, respect the W40 composite order identity; do not count an order once per line item.",
        "Define a small acyclic tree such as Net profit, Gross profit and expenses; keep multiplication under its actual parent.",
        "Compute current and previous-year measures with the same country scope and comparable date context.",
        "Apply the midpoint split to products and signed deltas to sums/subtractions.",
        "Verify child contributions equal each parent delta, including negative values and zero or missing PY.",
        "On focus as well as hover expose formula, definition and contribution; provide a linear readable alternative.",
        "Collapse/expand without changing filter context or losing the selected metric; disclose missing comparisons neutrally."
      ],
      "misconceptions_and_tradeoffs": [
        "Positive cost movement is numerically positive but may be commercially adverse.",
        "Do not manufacture zero percentage change when PY is zero or missing.",
        "An identity can reconcile perfectly while its business inputs are wrong."
      ],
      "practice": "A profit change is fully reconciled by order-volume and average-value effects. What further evidence is needed to explain why orders changed?",
      "model_feedback": "Reconciliation establishes the arithmetic. Investigate price changes, customer mix, campaigns, supply constraints or other supported evidence before attributing causality.",
      "prerequisite_slugs": [
        "decision-contract",
        "grain-and-ratios"
      ],
      "prerequisite_lesson_ids": [
        "lesson:creative:decision-contract",
        "lesson:creative:grain-and-ratios"
      ],
      "code_ids": [
        "code:creative:midpoint-contributions",
        "code:creative:safe-variance"
      ],
