    {
      "id": "lesson:creative:bump-chart",
      "slug": "bump-chart",
      "title": "Bump charts: explain rank movement and its population",
      "explanation": "A bump chart places rank on the quantitative axis and time on the ordered axis, with one series per entity. It communicates relative position, not the magnitude of the underlying metric. Ranking depends on the candidate population, current filters and tie policy. The W33 page describes a native line-chart approach but its field-well sentence places Program Name on Y and Rank in Legend; that wording conflicts with the intended rank chart. Preserve the source sentence in raw extraction and record the correction as a reviewer proposal. A defensible implementation instead tests time on X, numeric rank on Y, and program/entity as series, with rank 1 at the top.",
      "objectives": [
        "State the rank population and tie rule",
        "Distinguish stable rank from stable absolute performance"
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Inspect the pinned enrollment workbook: Year, ProgramName, StudentsEnrolled and 48 stored data rows.",
        "Aggregate enrollment per program/year at the required grain.",
        "Choose whether ranking compares all programs or only currently selected programs; document ALL versus ALLSELECTED semantics.",
        "Create an explicit rank measure and decide dense or skip tie handling.",
        "Bind time, numeric rank and entity to appropriate chart wells; verify actual output rather than following the source field-well typo.",
        "Reverse the rank direction only as needed so first place appears at the top; label the scale.",
        "Show underlying enrollment in a companion table or tooltip and include a non-hover equivalent.",
        "Test ties, missing years and filtered populations; do not interpolate missing facts as observed values."
      ],
      "misconceptions_and_tradeoffs": [
        "Rank changes can be caused by competitors moving even if an entity’s own value is unchanged.",
        "A ranking measure without a declared candidate set has ambiguous meaning."
      ],
      "practice": "Two programs both increase enrollment, but one drops in rank. Is the chart wrong?",
      "model_feedback": "Not necessarily. Other programs may grow faster, or the rank population may have changed. Compare absolute enrollment, eligible entities and tie policy.",
