    {
      "id": "lesson:creative:ternary",
      "slug": "ternary",
      "title": "Ternary cross-sell: three shares, one customer",
      "explanation": "A ternary point represents three nonnegative shares that sum to one. The W35 pattern uses one point per customer, aggregating order rows within the selected scope. The center means a balanced mix, not high revenue or good profit. A small specialist may be near the same vertex as a large specialist; include magnitude and exact values separately. Category shares with a zero or negative total, missing categories or returns require a defined policy before geometry. Source classification rules overlap at 60/20/20: both specialist and balanced conditions are met. A deterministic implementation must explicitly resolve the overlap rather than quietly pretending the source defines precedence.",
      "objectives": [
        "Validate the closure and grain of a composition",
        "Identify classification overlap and missing-denominator states"
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Use the linked W35 dataset reference; its OneDrive source is not bundled or asserted observed.",
        "Aggregate customer revenue/profit under Year, Country and Segment filters.",
        "Declare which three categories exhaust the denominator and exclude/flag invalid nonpositive totals.",
        "Compute three shares and verify nonnegativity and sum-to-one within an explicit numerical tolerance.",
        "Map shares to triangle coordinates only after validating the proportions.",
        "Adopt an explicit reviewed precedence for overlapping basket rules, or return a multi-label result until resolved.",
        "Use Top N under the active filters; show visible-cohort revenue coverage and exact values in a native matrix.",
        "Test matrix-to-point selection, keyboard access and a text/table alternative."
      ],
      "misconceptions_and_tradeoffs": [
        "The center is not a performance target.",
        "A point per order answers a different question from a point per customer.",
        "Negative-profit color overrides must not remove other category cues or rely on color alone."
      ],
      "practice": "A customer has category shares 60%, 20%, 20%. Which source archetype applies?",
      "model_feedback": "The published rules overlap. Flag this ambiguity and choose an explicitly reviewed precedence or a multi-label classification; neither single label is uniquely justified from the text alone.",
