    {
      "id": "lesson:creative:grain-and-ratios",
      "slug": "grain-and-ratios",
      "title": "Grain and denominators: the hidden structure of a chart",
      "explanation": "A chart encodes the measure supplied to it, including any grain error. Multi-line orders are not multiple distinct orders; customers are not rows. Ratios should be derived from coherent totals and denominators in the same context. An average of subgroup averages can disagree with the overall rate when groups have different sizes. In the linked financial exercise, order identity is a composite of Order ID, Order Date, Customer ID and Location ID. Preserve that scenario-specific rule; it is not a universal definition of an order key. Missing, zero and negative denominators have different interpretations and must not be flattened into a fabricated zero.",
      "objectives": [
        "Detect row-count inflation",
        "Explain a weighted total without inventing an observed result"
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Write one sentence specifying what one source row represents.",
        "List the unique business entity key and validate uniqueness at the intended aggregation.",
        "Construct numerator and denominator from compatible populations and periods.",
        "Calculate the overall ratio from totals; compare it with any displayed average-of-ratios.",
        "Test a subgroup with no eligible observations and a group with zero denominator.",
        "Keep the unit and denominator visible in tables, cards and tooltips."
      ],
      "misconceptions_and_tradeoffs": [
        "An invoice ID repeated on legitimate invoice lines is not automatically a duplicate defect.",
        "A mathematically defined ratio can still be a commercially inappropriate KPI."
      ],
      "practice": "A customer has multiple order lines. Why might COUNTROWS exaggerate the apparent customer opportunity?",
      "model_feedback": "It counts rows rather than customers or distinct orders. Aggregate to the declared customer grain and validate the order identity before deriving basket measures.",
      "prerequisite_slugs": [],
      "prerequisite_lesson_ids": [],
      "code_ids": [
        "code:creative:safe-variance"
      ],
