    {
      "id": "lesson:creative:visual-integrity",
      "slug": "visual-integrity",
      "title": "Scale choices change the argument",
      "explanation": "When bar length encodes a value, the baseline affects perceived ratios; cutting it can manufacture an exaggerated contrast. A line chart primarily encodes position and changes along an ordered sequence, so an appropriate range can differ, but it must still be disclosed and interpreted honestly. Logarithmic axes represent multiplicative ratios, not equal additive differences, and standard log axes cannot represent zero or negative values. Dual axes can create apparent relationships through scale selection. Choose comparable small multiples or indexed series when their interpretation better matches the question.",
      "objectives": [
        "Explain which encoding a scale supports",
        "Critique an apparent relationship without alleging unsupported causation"
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "State whether the task is magnitude comparison, change or multiplicative growth.",
        "Identify what length or position represents and inspect the baseline.",
        "Show units, transformation and any range restriction clearly.",
        "Compare an alternative with shared-scale panels or an explicitly defined index.",
        "Check zero/negative values before selecting a logarithmic encoding.",
        "Ask whether the conclusion survives a reasonable scale choice."
      ],
      "misconceptions_and_tradeoffs": [
        "Not every time variable implies a line chart.",
        "A chart with a technically legal axis can still mislead."
      ],
      "practice": "Two dual-axis series align after the axis limits are adjusted. What does that establish?",
      "model_feedback": "Only that the chosen scales make them align. It does not establish correlation stability or causation; inspect the actual values and analytical evidence.",
      "prerequisite_slugs": [
        "decision-contract"
      ],
      "prerequisite_lesson_ids": [
        "lesson:creative:decision-contract"
      ],
