    {
      "id": "lesson:creative:small-multiples",
      "slug": "small-multiples",
      "title": "Small multiples: keep comparison conditions visible",
      "explanation": "Faceting repeats a consistent view for groups, reducing overplotting and making local patterns easier to inspect. Comparability depends on units, axes, observation range and definitions. A panel with an independently scaled axis can be useful for shape, but it cannot support casual absolute-height comparison with a differently scaled panel. Small multiples also consume space; many tiny panels can be less readable than a targeted filter or summary table. The W39 pattern uses site panels specifically to contrast distributions under shared bin choices.",
      "objectives": [
        "Distinguish shape comparison from magnitude comparison",
        "Choose facet/filter trade-offs for a stated audience"
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Select a shared question, measure, time range and bin definition.",
        "Choose a manageable grouping dimension and define panel order.",
        "Use comparable scales when absolute comparison is required.",
        "Make sample size and missing-panel conditions visible.",
        "Test labels and keyboard access at the actual target viewport.",
        "Use filtering or a companion table when the panel count overwhelms the task."
      ],
      "misconceptions_and_tradeoffs": [
        "Matching chart types do not guarantee matching scales.",
        "More panels do not necessarily provide more useful information."
      ],
      "practice": "Two panels have equal-height peaks but different Y ranges. Can you infer equal peak counts?",
      "model_feedback": "No. Read the axes or use a shared count scale; equal height under different scales does not imply equal magnitude.",
