    {
      "id": "lesson:creative:uncertainty",
      "slug": "uncertainty",
      "title": "Distinguish description, association and causal evidence",
      "explanation": "A display can be accurate about the sample and still support an invalid inference. Check population, sampling, missingness and aggregation before generalizing. Association can change after stratification; aggregation can hide different group sizes and opposing subgroup patterns. A confidence interval expresses uncertainty under a statistical procedure and assumptions, not a guarantee about future individual cases. Statistical significance does not establish practical importance, and a p-value is not the probability that the null is true. Keep these interpretations distinct when generating explanatory cards or executive narratives.",
      "objectives": [
        "Qualify an association without claiming causality",
        "Explain why uncertainty and practical magnitude both matter"
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Identify observed data versus educational fixtures and state the eligible population.",
        "Inspect missingness and selection before comparing groups.",
        "Compare aggregate and subgroup patterns, including denominators and mix.",
        "Report effect magnitude and uncertainty using the method’s assumptions.",
        "Write a conclusion at the strongest level the design supports.",
        "Propose what additional design or data could challenge the conclusion."
      ],
      "misconceptions_and_tradeoffs": [
        "An important-looking chart is not proof of an important effect.",
        "A causal-sounding narrative framework cannot supply missing causal evidence."
      ],
      "practice": "A segment’s average delay is worse, but the segments use different route mixes. What should be investigated?",
      "model_feedback": "Stratify by meaningful routes and inspect group sizes, missingness and selection. The aggregate alone does not identify a segment-specific causal effect.",
      "prerequisite_slugs": [
        "distribution",
        "grain-and-ratios"
      ],
      "prerequisite_lesson_ids": [
        "lesson:creative:distribution",
        "lesson:creative:grain-and-ratios"
      ],
