    {
      "id": "lesson:creative:story-framework",
      "slug": "story-framework",
      "title": "Choose an argument shape after the evidence survives",
      "explanation": "Narrative frameworks organize a supported argument; they do not validate the data. The collection distinguishes a compressed Big Idea, an executive answer-first structure, contrast for a change proposal, and an analytical What/So what/Now what handoff. Keep source-specific attributions attached to the source rather than treating every framework label as an independently verified universal rule. A causal spine is dangerous if its “because” clauses exceed the study design. If no decision owner or meaningful action exists, an annotated chart may be a better deliverable than a dramatic story.",
      "objectives": [
        "Select a framework for a communication job",
        "Preserve uncertainty instead of completing an artificial narrative arc"
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Name the audience, decision and evidence threshold.",
        "Write a one-sentence finding with scope and uncertainty.",
        "Choose a format: short presentation, analytical report or reproducible notebook.",
        "Use an argument shape that fits diagnosis, choice or change; avoid adding a climax to weak evidence.",
        "Connect each recommendation to evidence and a decision owner.",
        "Include the most important counterevidence and what would falsify the claim."
      ],
      "misconceptions_and_tradeoffs": [
        "A persuasive structure does not establish causality.",
        "An insight headline must not hide an inconvenient denominator."
      ],
      "practice": "A manager needs a diagnostic memo, but a presenter proposes an emotional now-versus-future story. What should guide the choice?",
      "model_feedback": "Use the audience’s analytical job and decision need. Prefer a clear finding/evidence/next-action structure when diagnosis is the goal.",
      "prerequisite_slugs": [
        "uncertainty",
        "decision-contract"
      ],
      "prerequisite_lesson_ids": [
        "lesson:creative:uncertainty",
        "lesson:creative:decision-contract"
      ],
